// Armour, stones and the forge.
//
// Three sections, one file, because they are one system: a stone is both a
// socket filler and the forge's raw material, and a piece of gear is either
// bought or made out of stones. Splitting them across three files would mean
// three places to look when a tier does not line up.
//
// WHAT GEAR IS FOR. The run is a roguelite: relics are the interesting
// choices, and they are drawn at random inside a run and lost at the end of
// it. Gear is the opposite and deliberately so - bought and forged between
// runs, kept for good, and dull. Eight numbers that go up. It is what a player
// has to show for the twentieth run when the relics they found that time were
// bad ones.
//
// SLOTS. HEAD, BODY, HANDS, FEET, TRINKET. One of each may be worn, and none
// of them is drawn on the character: the pack has one body per hero and no
// layered equipment art. What a helmet changes is the numbers.
//
// TIERS. 1 to 4. Tier 1 is bought with the gold of a first stage clear; tier 4
// is forged out of tier 3 and a fistful of stones, and is not for sale at any
// price. The prices follow the same curve the weapon unlocks do, so a shelf of
// armour never out-competes a shelf of swords for the same gold.
//
// EFFECTS. Only names in ContentValidator.GEAR_EFFECTS, which is a shorter
// list than the relics get. Nothing here needs new combat code: Loadout folds
// gear into the same Modifiers relics and village upgrades already write to.
